1
00:00:03,318 --> 00:00:07,318
At the appointed time I returned to Miss Havisham’s, and my hesitating ring at

2
00:00:07,318 --> 00:00:13,200
the gate brought out Estella. She locked it after admitting me, as she had done before,

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:19,000
and again preceded me into the dark passage where her candle stood. She took no notice of

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:25,398
me until she had the candle in her hand, when she looked over her shoulder, superciliously saying,

5
00:00:25,398 --> 00:00:30,318
“You are to come this way to-day,” and took me to quite another part of the house.

6
00:00:31,439 --> 00:00:37,279
The passage was a long one, and seemed to pervade the whole square basement of the Manor House.

7
00:00:37,279 --> 00:00:42,439
We traversed but one side of the square, however, and at the end of it she stopped,

8
00:00:42,439 --> 00:00:50,279
and put her candle down and opened a door. Here, the daylight reappeared, and I found myself in

9
00:00:50,279 --> 00:00:56,638
a small paved courtyard, the opposite side of which was formed by a detached dwelling-house,

10
00:00:56,639 --> 00:01:00,478
that looked as if it had once belonged to the manager or head clerk of the extinct

11
00:01:00,478 --> 00:01:06,679
brewery. There was a clock in the outer wall of this house. Like the clock in Miss Havisham’s

12
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:12,120
room, and like Miss Havisham’s watch, it had stopped at twenty minutes to nine.

13
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:16,959
We went in at the door, which stood open, and into a gloomy room with a low ceiling,

14
00:01:16,959 --> 00:01:22,719
on the ground-floor at the back. There was some company in the room, and Estella said to me as

15
00:01:22,719 --> 00:01:30,840
she joined it, “You are to go and stand there boy, till you are wanted.” “There”, being the window,

16
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:37,879
I crossed to it, and stood “there,” in a very uncomfortable state of mind, looking out.

17
00:01:37,879 --> 00:01:43,000
It opened to the ground, and looked into a most miserable corner of the neglected garden,

18
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,120
upon a rank ruin of cabbage-stalks, and one box-tree that had been clipped round long ago,

19
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:55,799
like a pudding, and had a new growth at the top of it, out of shape and of a different colour, as if

20
00:01:55,799 --> 00:02:02,719
that part of the pudding had stuck to the saucepan and got burnt. This was my homely thought, as I

21
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:08,799
contemplated the box-tree. There had been some light snow, overnight, and it lay nowhere else to

22
00:02:08,799 --> 00:02:14,359
my knowledge; but, it had not quite melted from the cold shadow of this bit of garden, and the

23
00:02:14,359 --> 00:02:20,759
wind caught it up in little eddies and threw it at the window, as if it pelted me for coming there.

24
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:24,279
I divined that my coming had stopped conversation in the room,

25
00:02:24,279 --> 00:02:27,799
and that its other occupants were looking at me. I could see nothing

26
00:02:27,799 --> 00:02:31,478
of the room except the shining of the fire in the window-glass,

27
00:02:31,478 --> 00:02:37,799
but I stiffened in all my joints with the consciousness that I was under close inspection.

28
00:02:37,799 --> 00:02:43,520
There were three ladies in the room and one gentleman. Before I had been standing at the

29
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:49,040
window five minutes, they somehow conveyed to me that they were all toadies and humbugs,

30
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:53,719
but that each of them pretended not to know that the others were toadies and humbugs: because the

31
00:02:53,719 --> 00:02:59,799
admission that he or she did know it, would have made him or her out to be a toady and humbug.

32
00:02:59,799 --> 00:03:04,959
They all had a listless and dreary air of waiting somebody’s pleasure, and the most talkative of

33
00:03:04,959 --> 00:03:11,959
the ladies had to speak quite rigidly to repress a yawn. This lady, whose name was Camilla, very much

34
00:03:11,959 --> 00:03:17,600
reminded me of my sister, with the difference that she was older, and (as I found when I caught sight

35
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:24,439
of her) of a blunter cast of features. Indeed, when I knew her better I began to think it was

36
00:03:24,439 --> 00:03:30,439
a Mercy she had any features at all, so very blank and high was the dead wall of her face.

37
00:03:30,439 --> 00:03:32,799
“Poor dear soul!” said this lady,

38
00:03:32,799 --> 00:03:38,639
with an abruptness of manner quite my sister’s. “Nobody’s enemy but his own!”

39
00:03:38,639 --> 00:03:40,398
“It would be much more commendable to be

40
00:03:40,398 --> 00:03:45,638
somebody else’s enemy,” said the gentleman; “far more natural.”

41
00:03:45,639 --> 00:03:51,040
“Cousin Raymond,” observed another lady, “we are to love our neighbour.”

42
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:57,000
“Sarah Pocket,” returned Cousin Raymond, “if a man is not his own neighbour, who is?”

43
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,520
Miss Pocket laughed, and Camilla laughed and said (checking a yawn),

44
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:07,318
“The idea!” But I thought they seemed to think it rather a good idea too. The other lady,

45
00:04:07,318 --> 00:04:12,599
who had not spoken yet, said gravely and emphatically, “Very true!”

46
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:18,439
“Poor soul!” Camilla presently went on (I knew they had all been looking at me in the mean time),

47
00:04:18,439 --> 00:04:23,799
“he is so very strange! Would anyone believe that when Tom’s wife died,

48
00:04:23,799 --> 00:04:27,639
he actually could not be induced to see the importance of the children’s having the

49
00:04:27,639 --> 00:04:34,398
deepest of trimmings to their mourning? ‘Good Lord!’ says he, ‘Camilla, what can it signify

50
00:04:34,398 --> 00:04:42,000
so long as the poor bereaved little things are in black?’ So like Matthew! The idea!”

51
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,719
“Good points in him, good points in him,” said Cousin Raymond; “Heaven forbid I should deny

52
00:04:47,719 --> 00:04:54,879
good points in him; but he never had, and he never will have, any sense of the proprieties.”

53
00:04:54,879 --> 00:05:01,159
“You know I was obliged,” said Camilla,—“I was obliged to be firm. I said, ‘It WILL NOT DO,

54
00:05:01,159 --> 00:05:05,638
for the credit of the family.’ I told him that, without deep trimmings,

55
00:05:05,639 --> 00:05:10,920
the family was disgraced. I cried about it from breakfast till dinner. I injured

56
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:16,879
my digestion. And at last he flung out in his violent way, and said, with a D, ‘Then do as

57
00:05:16,879 --> 00:05:21,279
you like.’ Thank Goodness it will always be a consolation to me to know that I instantly went

58
00:05:21,279 --> 00:05:28,959
out in a pouring rain and bought the things.” “He paid for them, did he not?” asked Estella.

59
00:05:28,959 --> 00:05:33,760
“It’s not the question, my dear child, who paid for them,” returned Camilla. “I

60
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:40,600
bought them. And I shall often think of that with peace, when I wake up in the night.”

61
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:45,559
The ringing of a distant bell, combined with the echoing of some cry or call along the

62
00:05:45,559 --> 00:05:51,238
passage by which I had come, interrupted the conversation and caused Estella to say to me,

63
00:05:51,238 --> 00:05:56,920
“Now, boy!” On my turning round, they all looked at me with the utmost contempt,

64
00:05:56,920 --> 00:06:04,760
and, as I went out, I heard Sarah Pocket say, “Well I am sure! What next!” and Camilla add,

65
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:10,879
with indignation, “Was there ever such a fancy! The i-de-a!”

66
00:06:10,879 --> 00:06:16,159
As we were going with our candle along the dark passage, Estella stopped all of a sudden,

67
00:06:16,159 --> 00:06:21,719
and, facing round, said in her taunting manner, with her face quite close to mine,—

68
00:06:21,719 --> 00:06:23,200
“Well?”

69
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:28,359
“Well, miss?” I answered, almost falling over her and checking myself.

70
00:06:28,359 --> 00:06:32,318
She stood looking at me, and, of course, I stood looking at her.

71
00:06:32,318 --> 00:06:34,238
“Am I pretty?”

72
00:06:34,238 --> 00:06:36,879
“Yes; I think you are very pretty.”

73
00:06:36,879 --> 00:06:38,639
“Am I insulting?”

74
00:06:38,639 --> 00:06:41,799
“Not so much so as you were last time,” said I.

75
00:06:41,799 --> 00:06:43,359
“Not so much so?”

76
00:06:43,359 --> 00:06:44,359
“No.”

77
00:06:44,359 --> 00:06:47,040
She fired when she asked the last question,

78
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:51,799
and she slapped my face with such force as she had, when I answered it.

79
00:06:51,799 --> 00:06:57,600
“Now?” said she. “You little coarse monster, what do you think of me now?”

80
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:59,398
“I shall not tell you.”

81
00:06:59,398 --> 00:07:03,558
“Because you are going to tell upstairs. Is that it?”

82
00:07:03,559 --> 00:07:07,799
“No,” said I, “that’s not it.”

83
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:11,120
“Why don’t you cry again, you little wretch?”

84
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:15,879
“Because I’ll never cry for you again,” said I. Which was, I suppose,

85
00:07:15,879 --> 00:07:21,238
as false a declaration as ever was made; for I was inwardly crying for her then,

86
00:07:21,238 --> 00:07:24,879
and I know what I know of the pain she cost me afterwards.

87
00:07:24,879 --> 00:07:27,719
We went on our way upstairs after this episode;

88
00:07:27,719 --> 00:07:32,159
and, as we were going up, we met a gentleman groping his way down.

89
00:07:32,159 --> 00:07:36,760
“Whom have we here?” asked the gentleman, stopping and looking at me.

90
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:39,120
“A boy,” said Estella.

91
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:44,000
He was a burly man of an exceedingly dark complexion, with an exceedingly large head,

92
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,959
and a corresponding large hand. He took my chin in his large hand and turned up my face to have

93
00:07:49,959 --> 00:07:55,398
a look at me by the light of the candle. He was prematurely bald on the top of his head,

94
00:07:55,398 --> 00:08:00,638
and had bushy black eyebrows that wouldn’t lie down but stood up bristling. His eyes were

95
00:08:00,639 --> 00:08:08,159
set very deep in his head, and were disagreeably sharp and suspicious. He had a large watch-chain,

96
00:08:08,159 --> 00:08:11,879
and strong black dots where his beard and whiskers would have been if he had let

97
00:08:11,879 --> 00:08:17,719
them. He was nothing to me, and I could have had no foresight then, that he ever

98
00:08:17,719 --> 00:08:24,279
would be anything to me, but it happened that I had this opportunity of observing him well.

99
00:08:24,279 --> 00:08:28,599
“Boy of the neighbourhood? Hey?” said he.

100
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:29,760
“Yes, sir,” said I.

101
00:08:30,318 --> 00:08:32,318
“How do you come here?”

102
00:08:32,318 --> 00:08:35,279
“Miss Havisham sent for me, sir,” I explained.

103
00:08:35,279 --> 00:08:39,279
“Well! Behave yourself. I have a pretty large experience of boys,

104
00:08:39,279 --> 00:08:43,318
and you’re a bad set of fellows. Now mind!” said he,

105
00:08:43,318 --> 00:08:49,040
biting the side of his great forefinger as he frowned at me, “you behave yourself!”

106
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:53,879
With those words, he released me—which I was glad of, for his hand smelt of

107
00:08:53,879 --> 00:08:59,840
scented soap—and went his way downstairs. I wondered whether he could be a doctor;

108
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:05,120
but no, I thought; he couldn’t be a doctor, or he would have a quieter and more persuasive

109
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:10,760
manner. There was not much time to consider the subject, for we were soon in Miss Havisham’s room,

110
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:17,318
where she and everything else were just as I had left them. Estella left me standing near the door,

111
00:09:17,318 --> 00:09:22,359
and I stood there until Miss Havisham cast her eyes upon me from the dressing-table.

112
00:09:22,359 --> 00:09:30,120
“So!” she said, without being startled or surprised: “the days have worn away, have they?”

113
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:32,519
“Yes, ma’am. To-day is—”

114
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:35,000
“There, there, there!” with the impatient movement

115
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:40,520
of her fingers. “I don’t want to know. Are you ready to play?”

116
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:45,799
I was obliged to answer in some confusion, “I don’t think I am, ma’am.”

117
00:09:45,799 --> 00:09:50,559
“Not at cards again?” she demanded, with a searching look.

118
00:09:50,559 --> 00:09:54,760
“Yes, ma’am; I could do that, if I was wanted.”

119
00:09:54,760 --> 00:10:00,079
“Since this house strikes you old and grave, boy,” said Miss Havisham, impatiently,

120
00:10:00,079 --> 00:10:04,359
“and you are unwilling to play, are you willing to work?”

121
00:10:04,359 --> 00:10:07,398
I could answer this inquiry with a better heart than I had been able

122
00:10:07,398 --> 00:10:11,398
to find for the other question, and I said I was quite willing.

123
00:10:11,398 --> 00:10:15,079
“Then go into that opposite room,” said she, pointing at the door behind me with

124
00:10:15,079 --> 00:10:22,279
her withered hand, “and wait there till I come.” I crossed the staircase landing, and entered the

125
00:10:22,279 --> 00:10:28,318
room she indicated. From that room, too, the daylight was completely excluded, and it had an

126
00:10:28,318 --> 00:10:34,799
airless smell that was oppressive. A fire had been lately kindled in the damp old-fashioned grate,

127
00:10:34,799 --> 00:10:39,719
and it was more disposed to go out than to burn up, and the reluctant smoke which hung in the

128
00:10:39,719 --> 00:10:47,559
room seemed colder than the clearer air,—like our own marsh mist. Certain wintry branches of candles

129
00:10:47,559 --> 00:10:53,079
on the high chimney-piece faintly lighted the chamber; or it would be more expressive to say,

130
00:10:53,079 --> 00:10:59,639
faintly troubled its darkness. It was spacious, and I dare say had once been handsome,

131
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:06,120
but every discernible thing in it was covered with dust and mould, and dropping to pieces.

132
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:11,599
The most prominent object was a long table with a tablecloth spread on it, as if a feast had been

133
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:17,559
in preparation when the house and the clocks all stopped together. An epergne or centre-piece of

134
00:11:17,559 --> 00:11:22,639
some kind was in the middle of this cloth; it was so heavily overhung with cobwebs that its

135
00:11:22,639 --> 00:11:28,318
form was quite undistinguishable; and, as I looked along the yellow expanse out of which I remember

136
00:11:28,318 --> 00:11:35,238
its seeming to grow, like a black fungus, I saw speckle-legged spiders with blotchy bodies running

137
00:11:35,238 --> 00:11:41,000
home to it, and running out from it, as if some circumstances of the greatest public importance

138
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:47,719
had just transpired in the spider community. I heard the mice too, rattling behind the panels,

139
00:11:47,719 --> 00:11:52,520
as if the same occurrence were important to their interests. But the black beetles

140
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:58,318
took no notice of the agitation, and groped about the hearth in a ponderous elderly way,

141
00:11:58,318 --> 00:12:04,000
as if they were short-sighted and hard of hearing, and not on terms with one another.

142
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,439
These crawling things had fascinated my attention, and I was watching them from

143
00:12:08,439 --> 00:12:15,079
a distance, when Miss Havisham laid a hand upon my shoulder. In her other hand she had

144
00:12:15,079 --> 00:12:21,680
a crutch-headed stick on which she leaned, and she looked like the Witch of the place.

145
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:26,439
“This,” said she, pointing to the long table with her stick,

146
00:12:26,439 --> 00:12:33,599
“is where I will be laid when I am dead. They shall come and look at me here.”

147
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:38,359
With some vague misgiving that she might get upon the table then and there and die at once,

148
00:12:38,359 --> 00:12:45,359
the complete realisation of the ghastly waxwork at the Fair, I shrank under her touch.

149
00:12:45,359 --> 00:12:48,398
“What do you think that is?” she asked me,

150
00:12:48,398 --> 00:12:53,238
again pointing with her stick; “that, where those cobwebs are?”

151
00:12:53,238 --> 00:12:55,519
“I can’t guess what it is, ma’am.”

152
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:59,920
“It’s a great cake. A bride-cake. Mine!”

153
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:03,120
She looked all round the room in a glaring manner, and then said,

154
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:12,439
leaning on me while her hand twitched my shoulder, “Come, come, come! Walk me, walk me!”

155
00:13:12,439 --> 00:13:15,238
I made out from this, that the work I had to do,

156
00:13:15,238 --> 00:13:21,439
was to walk Miss Havisham round and round the room. Accordingly, I started at once,

157
00:13:21,439 --> 00:13:25,398
and she leaned upon my shoulder, and we went away at a pace that might have been

158
00:13:25,398 --> 00:13:32,478
an imitation (founded on my first impulse under that roof) of Mr. Pumblechook’s chaise-cart.

159
00:13:32,478 --> 00:13:38,519
She was not physically strong, and after a little time said, “Slower!” Still,

160
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:44,559
we went at an impatient fitful speed, and as we went, she twitched the hand upon my shoulder,

161
00:13:44,559 --> 00:13:49,478
and worked her mouth, and led me to believe that we were going fast because her thoughts

162
00:13:49,478 --> 00:13:56,359
went fast. After a while she said, “Call Estella!” so I went out on the landing

163
00:13:56,359 --> 00:14:02,120
and roared that name as I had done on the previous occasion. When her light appeared,

164
00:14:02,120 --> 00:14:07,680
I returned to Miss Havisham, and we started away again round and round the room.

165
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:11,000
If only Estella had come to be a spectator of our proceedings,

166
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:16,478
I should have felt sufficiently discontented; but as she brought with her the three ladies

167
00:14:16,478 --> 00:14:22,200
and the gentleman whom I had seen below, I didn’t know what to do. In my politeness,

168
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:28,000
I would have stopped; but Miss Havisham twitched my shoulder, and we posted on,—with

169
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:33,719
a shame-faced consciousness on my part that they would think it was all my doing.

170
00:14:33,719 --> 00:14:38,959
“Dear Miss Havisham,” said Miss Sarah Pocket. “How well you look!”

171
00:14:38,959 --> 00:14:44,520
“I do not,” returned Miss Havisham. “I am yellow skin and bone.”

172
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:47,840
Camilla brightened when Miss Pocket met with this rebuff;

173
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:52,639
and she murmured, as she plaintively contemplated Miss Havisham, “Poor dear

174
00:14:52,639 --> 00:14:58,439
soul! Certainly not to be expected to look well, poor thing. The idea!”

175
00:14:58,439 --> 00:15:03,638
“And how are you?” said Miss Havisham to Camilla. As we were close to Camilla then,

176
00:15:03,639 --> 00:15:08,920
I would have stopped as a matter of course, only Miss Havisham wouldn’t stop. We swept on,

177
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:12,200
and I felt that I was highly obnoxious to Camilla.

178
00:15:12,200 --> 00:15:17,120
“Thank you, Miss Havisham,” she returned, “I am as well as can be expected.”

179
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:22,478
“Why, what’s the matter with you?” asked Miss Havisham, with exceeding sharpness.

180
00:15:22,478 --> 00:15:27,759
“Nothing worth mentioning,” replied Camilla. “I don’t wish to make a display of my feelings,

181
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:31,879
but I have habitually thought of you more in the night than I am quite equal to.”

182
00:15:31,879 --> 00:15:38,879
“Then don’t think of me,” retorted Miss Havisham. “Very easily said!” remarked Camilla, amiably

183
00:15:38,879 --> 00:15:45,719
repressing a sob, while a hitch came into her upper lip, and her tears overflowed. “Raymond

184
00:15:45,719 --> 00:15:51,318
is a witness what ginger and sal volatile I am obliged to take in the night. Raymond is

185
00:15:51,318 --> 00:15:57,159
a witness what nervous jerkings I have in my legs. Chokings and nervous jerkings, however,

186
00:15:57,159 --> 00:16:02,680
are nothing new to me when I think with anxiety of those I love. If I could be less affectionate

187
00:16:02,680 --> 00:16:09,398
and sensitive, I should have a better digestion and an iron set of nerves. I am sure I wish it

188
00:16:09,398 --> 00:16:18,120
could be so. But as to not thinking of you in the night—The idea!” Here, a burst of tears.

189
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:22,680
The Raymond referred to, I understood to be the gentleman present, and him I understood

190
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:29,520
to be Mr. Camilla. He came to the rescue at this point, and said in a consolatory and complimentary

191
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:34,478
voice, “Camilla, my dear, it is well known that your family feelings are gradually

192
00:16:34,478 --> 00:16:39,599
undermining you to the extent of making one of your legs shorter than the other.”

193
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:45,120
“I am not aware,” observed the grave lady whose voice I had heard but once,

194
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:50,599
“that to think of any person is to make a great claim upon that person, my dear.”

195
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:56,680
Miss Sarah Pocket, whom I now saw to be a little dry, brown, corrugated old woman,

196
00:16:56,680 --> 00:17:01,520
with a small face that might have been made of walnut-shells, and a large mouth like

197
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:09,639
a cat’s without the whiskers, supported this position by saying, “No, indeed, my dear. Hem!”

198
00:17:09,639 --> 00:17:13,559
“Thinking is easy enough,” said the grave lady.

199
00:17:13,559 --> 00:17:17,879
“What is easier, you know?” assented Miss Sarah Pocket.

200
00:17:17,879 --> 00:17:22,200
“Oh, yes, yes!” cried Camilla, whose fermenting feelings appeared to rise

201
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:28,920
from her legs to her bosom. “It’s all very true! It’s a weakness to be so affectionate,

202
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:34,359
but I can’t help it. No doubt my health would be much better if it was otherwise,

203
00:17:34,359 --> 00:17:40,120
still I wouldn’t change my disposition if I could. It’s the cause of much suffering, but it’s

204
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:47,120
a consolation to know I possess it, when I wake up in the night.” Here another burst of feeling.

205
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:52,519
Miss Havisham and I had never stopped all this time, but kept going round and round the room;

206
00:17:52,520 --> 00:17:55,318
now brushing against the skirts of the visitors,

207
00:17:55,318 --> 00:17:59,238
now giving them the whole length of the dismal chamber.

208
00:17:59,238 --> 00:18:05,318
“There’s Matthew!” said Camilla. “Never mixing with any natural ties, never coming here to see

209
00:18:05,318 --> 00:18:11,359
how Miss Havisham is! I have taken to the sofa with my staylace cut, and have lain there hours

210
00:18:11,359 --> 00:18:18,478
insensible, with my head over the side, and my hair all down, and my feet I don’t know where—”

211
00:18:18,478 --> 00:18:22,200
(“Much higher than your head, my love,” said Mr. Camilla.)

212
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:26,279
“I have gone off into that state, hours and hours, on account of

213
00:18:26,279 --> 00:18:32,200
Matthew’s strange and inexplicable conduct, and nobody has thanked me.”

214
00:18:32,200 --> 00:18:37,120
“Really I must say I should think not!” interposed the grave lady.

215
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:42,238
“You see, my dear,” added Miss Sarah Pocket (a blandly vicious personage),

216
00:18:42,238 --> 00:18:47,359
“the question to put to yourself is, who did you expect to thank you, my love?”

217
00:18:47,359 --> 00:18:52,359
“Without expecting any thanks, or anything of the sort,” resumed Camilla, “I have remained in

218
00:18:52,359 --> 00:18:58,200
that state, hours and hours, and Raymond is a witness of the extent to which I have choked,

219
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:03,478
and what the total inefficacy of ginger has been, and I have been heard at the piano-forte

220
00:19:03,478 --> 00:19:08,040
tuner’s across the street, where the poor mistaken children have even supposed it to

221
00:19:08,040 --> 00:19:16,200
be pigeons cooing at a distance,—and now to be told—” Here Camilla put her hand to her throat,

222
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:21,959
and began to be quite chemical as to the formation of new combinations there.

223
00:19:21,959 --> 00:19:27,279
When this same Matthew was mentioned, Miss Havisham stopped me and herself, and stood looking

224
00:19:27,279 --> 00:19:34,398
at the speaker. This change had a great influence in bringing Camilla’s chemistry to a sudden end.

225
00:19:34,398 --> 00:19:40,799
“Matthew will come and see me at last,” said Miss Havisham, sternly, “when I am laid on that table.

226
00:19:40,799 --> 00:19:47,398
That will be his place,—there,” striking the table with her stick, “at my head! And yours

227
00:19:47,398 --> 00:19:54,318
will be there! And your husband’s there! And Sarah Pocket’s there! And Georgiana’s there!

228
00:19:54,318 --> 00:20:01,120
Now you all know where to take your stations when you come to feast upon me. And now go!”

229
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:04,439
At the mention of each name, she had struck the table with her stick

230
00:20:04,439 --> 00:20:11,558
in a new place. She now said, “Walk me, walk me!” and we went on again.

231
00:20:11,559 --> 00:20:17,760
“I suppose there’s nothing to be done,” exclaimed Camilla, “but comply and depart. It’s something to

232
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:23,478
have seen the object of one’s love and duty for even so short a time. I shall think of it with a

233
00:20:23,478 --> 00:20:28,959
melancholy satisfaction when I wake up in the night. I wish Matthew could have that comfort,

234
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:36,359
but he sets it at defiance. I am determined not to make a display of my feelings,

235
00:20:36,359 --> 00:20:40,519
but it’s very hard to be told one wants to feast on one’s relations,—as if one

236
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:50,760
was a Giant,—and to be told to go. The bare idea!” Mr. Camilla interposing, as Mrs. Camilla laid her

237
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:56,879
hand upon her heaving bosom, that lady assumed an unnatural fortitude of manner which I supposed to

238
00:20:56,879 --> 00:21:02,398
be expressive of an intention to drop and choke when out of view, and kissing her hand to Miss

239
00:21:02,398 --> 00:21:10,359
Havisham, was escorted forth. Sarah Pocket and Georgiana contended who should remain last; but

240
00:21:10,359 --> 00:21:16,359
Sarah was too knowing to be outdone, and ambled round Georgiana with that artful slipperiness that

241
00:21:16,359 --> 00:21:22,478
the latter was obliged to take precedence. Sarah Pocket then made her separate effect of departing

242
00:21:22,478 --> 00:21:29,120
with, “Bless you, Miss Havisham dear!” and with a smile of forgiving pity on her walnut-shell

243
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:34,680
countenance for the weaknesses of the rest. While Estella was away lighting them down,

244
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:39,760
Miss Havisham still walked with her hand on my shoulder, but more and more slowly.

245
00:21:39,760 --> 00:21:45,639
At last she stopped before the fire, and said, after muttering and looking at it some seconds,—

246
00:21:45,639 --> 00:21:48,040
“This is my birthday, Pip.”

247
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:52,680
I was going to wish her many happy returns, when she lifted her stick.

248
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:57,439
“I don’t suffer it to be spoken of. I don’t suffer those who were here just now,

249
00:21:57,439 --> 00:22:04,199
or any one to speak of it. They come here on the day, but they dare not refer to it.”

250
00:22:04,199 --> 00:22:07,478
Of course I made no further effort to refer to it.

251
00:22:07,478 --> 00:22:13,759
“On this day of the year, long before you were born, this heap of decay,”

252
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:19,279
stabbing with her crutched stick at the pile of cobwebs on the table, but not touching it,

253
00:22:19,279 --> 00:22:25,558
“was brought here. It and I have worn away together. The mice have gnawed at it,

254
00:22:25,559 --> 00:22:28,840
and sharper teeth than teeth of mice have gnawed at me.”

255
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,359
She held the head of her stick against her heart as she stood looking at the table;

256
00:22:34,359 --> 00:22:39,478
she in her once white dress, all yellow and withered; the once white cloth all

257
00:22:39,478 --> 00:22:45,040
yellow and withered; everything around in a state to crumble under a touch.

258
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:50,279
“When the ruin is complete,” said she, with a ghastly look, “and when they lay me dead,

259
00:22:50,279 --> 00:22:55,359
in my bride’s dress on the bride’s table,—which shall be done, and

260
00:22:55,359 --> 00:23:02,079
which will be the finished curse upon him,—so much the better if it is done on this day!”

261
00:23:02,079 --> 00:23:07,680
She stood looking at the table as if she stood looking at her own figure lying there. I remained

262
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:15,520
quiet. Estella returned, and she too remained quiet. It seemed to me that we continued thus for

263
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:22,040
a long time. In the heavy air of the room, and the heavy darkness that brooded in its remoter

264
00:23:22,040 --> 00:23:28,760
corners, I even had an alarming fancy that Estella and I might presently begin to decay.

265
00:23:30,040 --> 00:23:34,920
At length, not coming out of her distraught state by degrees, but in an instant,

266
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:42,000
Miss Havisham said, “Let me see you two play cards; why have you not begun?” With that,

267
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:50,159
we returned to her room, and sat down as before; I was beggared, as before; and again, as before,

268
00:23:50,159 --> 00:23:55,398
Miss Havisham watched us all the time, directed my attention to Estella’s beauty,

269
00:23:55,398 --> 00:24:00,879
and made me notice it the more by trying her jewels on Estella’s breast and hair.

270
00:24:00,879 --> 00:24:06,439
Estella, for her part, likewise treated me as before, except that she did not condescend to

271
00:24:06,439 --> 00:24:12,040
speak. When we had played some half-dozen games, a day was appointed for my return,

272
00:24:12,040 --> 00:24:17,079
and I was taken down into the yard to be fed in the former dog-like manner. There,

273
00:24:17,079 --> 00:24:20,519
too, I was again left to wander about as I liked.

274
00:24:20,520 --> 00:24:25,159
It is not much to the purpose whether a gate in that garden wall which I had scrambled up to

275
00:24:25,159 --> 00:24:32,478
peep over on the last occasion was, on that last occasion, open or shut. Enough that I saw no gate

276
00:24:32,478 --> 00:24:39,120
then, and that I saw one now. As it stood open, and as I knew that Estella had let the visitors

277
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:45,760
out,—for she had returned with the keys in her hand,—I strolled into the garden, and strolled

278
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:52,079
all over it. It was quite a wilderness, and there were old melon-frames and cucumber-frames in it,

279
00:24:52,079 --> 00:24:56,558
which seemed in their decline to have produced a spontaneous growth of weak attempts at pieces

280
00:24:56,559 --> 00:25:04,478
of old hats and boots, with now and then a weedy offshoot into the likeness of a battered saucepan.

281
00:25:04,478 --> 00:25:08,918
When I had exhausted the garden and a greenhouse with nothing in it but a fallen-down grape-vine

282
00:25:08,920 --> 00:25:13,398
and some bottles, I found myself in the dismal corner upon which I had looked

283
00:25:13,398 --> 00:25:19,159
out of the window. Never questioning for a moment that the house was now empty,

284
00:25:19,159 --> 00:25:23,840
I looked in at another window, and found myself, to my great surprise,

285
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:28,639
exchanging a broad stare with a pale young gentleman with red eyelids and light hair.

286
00:25:30,079 --> 00:25:36,000
This pale young gentleman quickly disappeared, and reappeared beside me. He had been at his

287
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:41,199
books when I had found myself staring at him, and I now saw that he was inky.

288
00:25:41,199 --> 00:25:44,679
“Halloa!” said he, “young fellow!”

289
00:25:44,680 --> 00:25:49,439
Halloa being a general observation which I had usually observed to be best answered by itself,

290
00:25:49,439 --> 00:25:54,318
I said, “Halloa!” politely omitting young fellow.

291
00:25:54,318 --> 00:25:56,439
“Who let you in?” said he.

292
00:25:56,439 --> 00:25:58,120
“Miss Estella.”

293
00:25:58,120 --> 00:26:00,799
“Who gave you leave to prowl about?”

294
00:26:00,799 --> 00:26:02,639
“Miss Estella.”

295
00:26:02,639 --> 00:26:06,000
“Come and fight,” said the pale young gentleman.

296
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:11,520
What could I do but follow him? I have often asked myself the question since; but what else

297
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:18,040
could I do? His manner was so final, and I was so astonished, that I followed where he led,

298
00:26:18,040 --> 00:26:23,600
as if I had been under a spell. “Stop a minute, though,” he said,

299
00:26:23,600 --> 00:26:28,559
wheeling round before we had gone many paces. “I ought to give you a reason for fighting, too.

300
00:26:29,439 --> 00:26:35,838
There it is!” In a most irritating manner he instantly slapped his hands against one another,

301
00:26:35,840 --> 00:26:41,680
daintily flung one of his legs up behind him, pulled my hair, slapped his hands again,

302
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:48,439
dipped his head, and butted it into my stomach. The bull-like proceeding last mentioned, besides

303
00:26:48,439 --> 00:26:53,478
that it was unquestionably to be regarded in the light of a liberty, was particularly disagreeable

304
00:26:53,478 --> 00:26:58,959
just after bread and meat. I therefore hit out at him and was going to hit out again,

305
00:26:58,959 --> 00:27:04,238
when he said, “Aha! Would you?” and began dancing backwards and forwards

306
00:27:04,238 --> 00:27:08,840
in a manner quite unparalleled within my limited experience.

307
00:27:08,840 --> 00:27:14,760
“Laws of the game!” said he. Here, he skipped from his left leg on to his right. “Regular

308
00:27:14,760 --> 00:27:20,559
rules!” Here, he skipped from his right leg on to his left. “Come to the ground,

309
00:27:20,559 --> 00:27:25,959
and go through the preliminaries!” Here, he dodged backwards and forwards,

310
00:27:25,959 --> 00:27:29,879
and did all sorts of things while I looked helplessly at him.

311
00:27:29,879 --> 00:27:35,478
I was secretly afraid of him when I saw him so dexterous; but I felt morally and physically

312
00:27:35,478 --> 00:27:41,000
convinced that his light head of hair could have had no business in the pit of my stomach,

313
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:46,680
and that I had a right to consider it irrelevant when so obtruded on my attention. Therefore,

314
00:27:46,680 --> 00:27:52,000
I followed him without a word, to a retired nook of the garden, formed by the junction of

315
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:58,478
two walls and screened by some rubbish. On his asking me if I was satisfied with the ground,

316
00:27:58,478 --> 00:28:04,799
and on my replying Yes, he begged my leave to absent himself for a moment, and quickly returned

317
00:28:04,799 --> 00:28:11,799
with a bottle of water and a sponge dipped in vinegar. “Available for both,” he said,

318
00:28:11,799 --> 00:28:18,000
placing these against the wall. And then fell to pulling off, not only his jacket and waistcoat,

319
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:24,840
but his shirt too, in a manner at once light-hearted, business-like, and bloodthirsty.

320
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:29,959
Although he did not look very healthy,—having pimples on his face, and a breaking out at his

321
00:28:29,959 --> 00:28:37,359
mouth,—these dreadful preparations quite appalled me. I judged him to be about my own age, but he

322
00:28:37,359 --> 00:28:43,519
was much taller, and he had a way of spinning himself about that was full of appearance. For

323
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:49,920
the rest, he was a young gentleman in a grey suit (when not denuded for battle), with his elbows,

324
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:56,079
knees, wrists, and heels considerably in advance of the rest of him as to development.

325
00:28:56,079 --> 00:29:01,199
My heart failed me when I saw him squaring at me with every demonstration of mechanical nicety,

326
00:29:01,199 --> 00:29:06,398
and eyeing my anatomy as if he were minutely choosing his bone. I never have been so

327
00:29:06,398 --> 00:29:12,238
surprised in my life, as I was when I let out the first blow, and saw him lying on his back,

328
00:29:12,238 --> 00:29:17,040
looking up at me with a bloody nose and his face exceedingly fore-shortened.

329
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:21,120
But, he was on his feet directly, and after sponging himself with a great show

330
00:29:21,120 --> 00:29:28,639
of dexterity began squaring again. The second greatest surprise I have ever had in my life

331
00:29:28,639 --> 00:29:33,639
was seeing him on his back again, looking up at me out of a black eye.

332
00:29:33,639 --> 00:29:38,920
His spirit inspired me with great respect. He seemed to have no strength, and he never once

333
00:29:38,920 --> 00:29:45,520
hit me hard, and he was always knocked down; but he would be up again in a moment, sponging

334
00:29:45,520 --> 00:29:50,559
himself or drinking out of the water-bottle, with the greatest satisfaction in seconding himself

335
00:29:50,559 --> 00:29:55,840
according to form, and then came at me with an air and a show that made me believe he really

336
00:29:55,840 --> 00:30:02,000
was going to do for me at last. He got heavily bruised, for I am sorry to record that the more

337
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:08,159
I hit him, the harder I hit him; but he came up again and again and again, until at last he got

338
00:30:08,159 --> 00:30:14,599
a bad fall with the back of his head against the wall. Even after that crisis in our affairs, he

339
00:30:14,600 --> 00:30:21,199
got up and turned round and round confusedly a few times, not knowing where I was; but finally went

340
00:30:21,199 --> 00:30:28,318
on his knees to his sponge and threw it up: at the same time panting out, “That means you have won.”

341
00:30:28,840 --> 00:30:34,199
He seemed so brave and innocent, that although I had not proposed the contest,

342
00:30:34,199 --> 00:30:39,959
I felt but a gloomy satisfaction in my victory. Indeed, I go so far as to hope

343
00:30:39,959 --> 00:30:44,760
that I regarded myself while dressing as a species of savage young wolf or other wild

344
00:30:44,760 --> 00:30:51,840
beast. However, I got dressed, darkly wiping my sanguinary face at intervals, and I said,

345
00:30:51,840 --> 00:31:00,520
“Can I help you?” and he said “No thankee,” and I said “Good afternoon,” and he said “Same to you.”

346
00:31:00,520 --> 00:31:06,398
When I got into the courtyard, I found Estella waiting with the keys. But she neither asked me

347
00:31:06,398 --> 00:31:12,439
where I had been, nor why I had kept her waiting; and there was a bright flush upon her face,

348
00:31:12,439 --> 00:31:16,718
as though something had happened to delight her. Instead of going straight to the gate,

349
00:31:16,719 --> 00:31:20,318
too, she stepped back into the passage, and beckoned me.

350
00:31:20,318 --> 00:31:23,840
“Come here! You may kiss me, if you like.”

351
00:31:23,840 --> 00:31:28,398
I kissed her cheek as she turned it to me. I think I would have gone through a great deal

352
00:31:28,398 --> 00:31:33,799
to kiss her cheek. But I felt that the kiss was given to the coarse common boy as a piece of money

353
00:31:33,799 --> 00:31:39,719
might have been, and that it was worth nothing. What with the birthday visitors,

354
00:31:39,719 --> 00:31:45,879
and what with the cards, and what with the fight, my stay had lasted so long,

355
00:31:45,879 --> 00:31:51,040
that when I neared home the light on the spit of sand off the point on the marshes was gleaming

356
00:31:51,040 --> 00:31:58,238
against a black night-sky, and Joe’s furnace was flinging a path of fire across the road.
